New-Post "what-to-pack-safari" "Safari Packing Checklist: What Works, What Stays Home" "Travel Tips" "travel-tips" "2026-08-05" "TZ" "Neutral layers, broken-in boots, binoculars per person, soft duffel for bush flights - plus the leave-home list." "<p class=`"lead`">Pack for 15-20kg soft duffels even if lodges store your hard case: bush flights are strict and most trips include one.</p><h2>Clothing that works</h2><p>Khaki, olive, grey - never white (dust) or navy/black (tsetse). Three to four shirts, convertible trousers, fleece plus packable down (mornings hit 5C June-August), wide-brim hat, buff. Laundry at most lodges returns next day - pack half what you think.</p><h2>Footwear and gear</h2><p>Broken-in walking shoes plus sandals. Binoculars per person - non-negotiable, sharing ruins sightings. Camera with 100-400mm zoom, dust bags, power bank. Headlamp for camps and night walks.</p><h2>Medical and documents</h2><p>DEET repellent, sunscreen, antihistamines, rehydration salts, prophylaxis per doctor. Passport copies in phone plus paper; insurance details printed - camps ask for them.</p><h2>Leave home</h2><p>Camouflage (restricted in some countries), drones (banned in most parks), flash expectations, rigid itineraries. Trekkers add route-specific kit: <a href=""/kilimanjaro/packing-list/"">Kilimanjaro list</a>, <a href=""/activities/gorilla-trekking/"">gorilla gear</a>.</p>" @(@{"q"="How strict are bush-flight luggage limits?";"a"="Strict: 15-20kg in soft duffels, weighed at check-in. Excess rides separately for a fee or stays behind - lodges store hard cases free."},@{"q"="What colors must we avoid?";"a"="White shows dust instantly; dark navy and black attract tsetse flies. Khaki, olive, stone and grey work everywhere."},@{"q"="Do lodges do laundry?";"a"="Almost all, usually overnight return. Pack for 3-4 days regardless of trip length - except intimates."},@{"q"="Binoculars or just camera?";"a"="Both: binoculars for finding, camera for keeping. One pair per person minimum."},@{"q"="Power and charging?";"a"="Camps charge via solar/mains with downtime midday; bring a power bank and multi-plug. Confirm inverter access on camping trips."},@{"q"="Kids packing extras?";"a"="Junior binoculars, wildlife checklist books, headlamp, extra snacks. Guides love briefing sharp-eyed kids."}) @(@("/tanzania-safari/5-day-tanzania-safari/","5-Day Tanzania","5 days","From $2,150","Classic circuit."),@("/kilimanjaro/lemosho-route/","Lemosho 8-Day","8 days","From $3,400","Highest success.")) @(@("/travel-guide/what-to-pack-for-safari/","Packing Guide","The full list."),@("/blog/how-many-days-safari/","Duration Math","Days to pack for."))
New-Post "best-time-visit-tanzania" "Best Time to Visit Tanzania: Month-by-Month Safari Guide" "Tanzania Safari" "tanzania-safari" "2026-08-20" "TZ" "June-October dry season and crossings, January-March calving, November-December value - matched to Migration zones." "<p class=`"lead`">Tanzania has no single best month - only the best month for your goal. Dry concentrates game; green cuts prices and crowds.</p><h2>June to October: dry season</h2><p>Best general game viewing; Grumeti crossings June, Mara River crossings July-October in the north. Peak prices, peak availability pressure - book 9-12 months ahead.</p><h2>January to March: calving</h2><p>Ndutu and the southern plains: 8,000 calves daily with lions, cheetahs and hyenas hunting in daylight. Fewer vehicles, superb photography, strong value.</p><h2>November to December: short rains value</h2><p>Green landscapes, migratory birds, newborn antelope, empty sightings. Brief afternoon storms, rarely trip-ruining.</p><h2>April to May: long rains</h2><p>The one genuinely limiting window: some murram roads impassable, a few camps close. Deep discounts for flexible travelers on southern circuits.</p>" @(@{"q"="Which month for river crossings?";"a"="Grumeti in June; Mara River July to October. Exact days follow rain - three-plus nights near the river converts odds to probability."},@{"q"="Is January good?";"a"="Superb for calving in Ndutu plus clear Kilimanjaro views in Amboseli if combining Kenya. Fewer crowds than August."},@{"q"="Can we travel with school holidays?";"a"="June-August and December-January align beautifully with dry or calving seasons. Book family rooms 9-12 months out."},@{"q"="What about Zanzibar timing?";"a"="Beach is year-round; June-October and January-March driest. Safari season and beach season agree - convenient."},@{"q"="How far ahead to book?";"a"="Migration months 9-12 months; calving 6-9; green season 2-4. Gorilla-style permit pressure does not apply - camps are the constraint."},@{"q"="Does it rain all day in April?";"a"="Rarely: intense bursts, often afternoons, with bright intervals. The issue is roads, not skies - southern black-cotton soil suffers most."}) @(@("/tanzania-migration-safari/7-day-migration-safari/","7-Day Migration Safari","7 days","From $3,800","Timed to your month."),@("/tanzania-safari/5-day-tanzania-safari/","5-Day Tanzania","5 days","From $2,150","Classic circuit.")) @(@("/blog/best-time-great-migration/","Migration Timing","Crossings vs calving."),@("/great-migration/month-by-month/","Month by Month","Herd positions."))
New-Post "kenya-first-timer-guide" "Kenya Safari for First-Timers: Mara, Amboseli & Lakes in 7 Days" "Kenya Safari" "kenya-safari" "2026-09-01" "KE" "A first-timer's Kenya blueprint: eTA before flying, Wilson Airport hops, three Mara nights, Amboseli dawns, Naivasha breaks." "<p class=`"lead`">Kenya is the easiest first safari in Africa: one international airport, short internal hops, and the Mara's density doing the heavy lifting.</p><h2>Before you fly</h2><p>Apply for the eTA online (typically 72 hours); yellow fever certificate if transiting endemic zones; prophylaxis per doctor. Pack for 15kg bush-flight limits even on road loops - internal hops creep in.</p><h2>The classic first week</h2><p>Day 1 Nairobi arrival plus Sheldrick elephants. Days 2-3 Amboseli tuskers and Kili dawns. Day 4 Naivasha boats and Crescent Island walks. Days 5-7 Masai Mara - three nights minimum for cats and crossings. Fly the long legs, drive the scenic ones.</p><h2>Mistakes to skip</h2><p>Two Mara nights (too few), reserve-gate day trips in August (crowds), skipping travel insurance, and overpacking hard cases. Private vehicle removes the minibus lottery.</p>" @(@{"q"="Is Kenya safe for first-timers?";"a"="Yes on standard circuits with pre-booked transfers and vetted camps. Nairobi needs normal big-city sense - we brief every arrival."},@{"q"="Fly or drive to the Mara?";"a"="Fly under five days; road for week loops where Naivasha and Nakuru are part of the experience. Hybrids split the difference."},@{"q"="What will we actually see?";"a"="Guaranteed nothing, likely everything: Mara cats daily in season, Amboseli herds reliably, rhinos at Nakuru, flamingo flocks shifting by lake levels."},@{"q"="How much spending money?";"a"="300-600 dollars per week covers drinks, souvenirs and tips. Cards work in cities; cash for tips and markets."},@{"q"="Can we add beach?";"a"="Direct Mara-to-Diani flights make 3 beach nights seamless. Safari first, sand after."},@{"q"="What about kids?";"a"="Superb first family safari: short hops, boats and walks between drives, strong kids programs. Under-fives need private vehicles and pool stops."}) @(@("/kenya-safari/7-day-kenya-safari/","7-Day Kenya Safari","7 days","From $3,100","The classic first week."),@("/kenya-safari/3-day-masai-mara-safari/","3-Day Mara Express","3 days","From $1,450","Maximum Mara fast.")) @(@("/blog/serengeti-vs-masai-mara/","Mara vs Serengeti","Pick your side."),@("/blog/how-many-days-safari/","Duration Math","How long to go."))
New-Post "gorilla-permit-guide" "Gorilla Permits Explained: Cost, Booking Windows & Sector Strategy" "Gorilla Trekking" "gorilla-trekking" "2026-09-10" "UG" "Uganda 800 vs Rwanda 1,500 dollars, 6-12 month booking windows, Bwindi sector strategy - permits first, lodges second." "<p class=`"lead`">Permits are the constraint around which entire trips are built. Understand them and everything else follows.</p><h2>Cost and what it funds</h2><p>Uganda 800 dollars, Rwanda 1,500 per person per trek. Fees fund rangers, trackers, veterinary units and community revenue-share - the conservation model that saved the species.</p><h2>Booking windows</h2><p>Peak months (Jul-Sep, Dec-Feb) sell out 6-12 months ahead in both countries. Shoulder months work at 3-4 months. We check live availability before quoting lodges - never the reverse.</p><h2>Bwindi sector strategy</h2><p>Rushaga and Nkuringo host the most families (permit odds); Buhoma has the deepest lodge bench. Match sector to dates first, lodge second - cross-sector transfers waste trek mornings.</p><h2>Rules that matter</h2><p>Minimum age 15, one hour with the family, 8-meter rule, no flash, masks near gorillas, stay home if ill (reschedules beat infections). Porters mandatory in our planning.</p>" @(@{"q"="Can permits sell out?";"a"="Routinely in peak months. Flexibility on sector and weekday unlocks availability; fixed dates need early booking."},@{"q"="What if it rains?";"a"="Treks run rain or shine - ponchos provided, trails slippery, gorillas unconcerned. Porters earn their fee."},@{"q"="One trek or two?";"a"="One saturates most travelers. Photographers and devotees take two; others spend the second permit on chimps."},@{"q"="Are permits refundable?";"a"="Partially per authority policy, with rescheduling options for illness. Terms arrive in writing before payment."},@{"q"="Rwanda worth the premium?";"a"="For speed and lodge splendor, yes. For cost and combinations, Uganda. Same hour, different logistics."},@{"q"="How do we pay?";"a"="Permit fees flow through our ground allocation with receipts. Never buy permits from unverified resellers."}) @(@("/uganda-safari/3-day-gorilla-trekking/","3-Day Gorilla Trek","3 days","From $2,650","Permits-first booking."),@("/uganda-safari/7-day-uganda-safari/","7-Day Primates + Wildlife","7 days","From $4,100","Full arc.")) @(@("/blog/uganda-vs-rwanda-gorillas/","Uganda vs Rwanda","Country choice."),@("/activities/gorilla-trekking/","Trekking Guide","Fitness and rules."))
